00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.409
Abraham: There are many terms that we will
discuss throughout this course that are

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:08.740
used in cyber security and some of them
we hear more often than others let’s look

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.699
at some of the most common used terms.

00:00:11.210 --> 00:00:14.870
Nicole: Threat -as we have already
discussed is the ability of hackers

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:19.490
or threat actors to target and
adversely impact organisational

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.230
systems, operations, and assets.

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:28.089
Threats take many different forms such as
malware, data breaches and DoS attacks.

00:00:28.820 --> 00:00:31.860
Abraham: Vulnerabilities-refers
to any week spot within the cyber

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.630
security of an organisation that
allows anyone to enter their systems

00:00:35.630 --> 00:00:40.420
and steal anything of value like data
that may compromise and adversely

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:42.550
impact an organisations operation.

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:47.510
Hackers use these in various ways
to gain access and attack a system.

00:00:48.139 --> 00:00:51.260
Vulnerabilities are also
known as the attack surface.

00:00:51.910 --> 00:00:55.959
Nicole: Software flaws are some of the
most common vulnerabilities such as,

00:00:56.039 --> 00:01:01.330
bugs or backdoors which enable access
by bypassing normal security measures.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:06.520
These may be as a result of a poor
system design processes or mistakes

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:10.750
made when the system was set up and
can go undetected for some time.

00:01:11.359 --> 00:01:15.080
User error can make a system
vulnerable for example when the user

00:01:15.120 --> 00:01:18.740
unwittingly clicks on a malicious
link in an email or text message.

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:23.390
They may also consistently use the
same obvious email for everything

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:25.919
easily guessed by the hacker.

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:31.670
Hackers can easily trick users into
giving away information by a variety

00:01:31.670 --> 00:01:33.170
of different phishing attacks.

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:37.320
Abraham: When an attack happens on the
day a weakness is discovered in software

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:39.870
this is classed as a zero day attack.

00:01:40.789 --> 00:01:43.470
This is because the hackers are
aware of the flaw and launch

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:48.010
their attack before the software
company can release the patch.

00:01:48.709 --> 00:01:52.850
Nicole: Risk- refers to the level
of impact a cyber attack may have on

00:01:52.850 --> 00:01:56.960
an organisation and its operations
for example financial loss,

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:59.740
disruption, and damage to reputation.

00:02:00.219 --> 00:02:04.390
It usually involves a combination of
threat, vulnerability, and impact.

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.519
Organisations may face a greater
risk if they have more valuable

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.570
information or financial assets.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.370
Add to that a flaw or a vulnerability
then the risk of attack would be high.

00:02:15.990 --> 00:02:19.190
Malicious threat actors also
like to target companies and

00:02:19.190 --> 00:02:23.549
organisations they don’t like so
as to damage their reputations.

00:02:24.809 --> 00:02:27.270
Abraham: Exploits- are codes
that take advantage of a

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.030
software vulnerability or flaw.

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:34.140
They allow unauthorised access
remotely, gaining elevated privileges

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.729
or to move deeper into the network.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:41.290
Exploit tools can also be embedded
into compromised webpages and as

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:45.130
the user navigates through them
the tool scans their device for

00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.500
vulnerabilities and inserts the malware.

00:02:48.110 --> 00:02:51.270
They can also be used as part
of a multi-component attack.

